error_explanation:
The separability condition demanded in this step is stated with a bare (non-absolute-valued) difference and without the hypothesis $\mathcal{V}^{(i)}
eq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}$, and as written it is impossible to satisfy. (1) The left-hand side $	ext{LHS}(i,k;j,l)=(W^{(V)}X^{(i)})\sigma_S[\cdots X_{:,k}^{(i)}\cdots]-(W^{(V)}X^{(j)})\sigma_S[\cdots X_{:,l}^{(j)}\cdots]$ is antisymmetric under swapping $(i,k)\leftrightarrow(j,l)$. If $	ext{LHS}(i,k;j,l)>\epsilon$, then the same required inequality applied to the swapped indices gives $	ext{LHS}(j,l;i,k)=-	ext{LHS}(i,k;j,l)>\epsilon$, i.e. $	ext{LHS}(i,k;j,l)<-\epsilon$, a direct contradiction; hence no such $\epsilon>0$ can exist. The fix is to wrap the LHS in absolute-value bars $|\cdots|>\epsilon$, which is symmetric and consistent. (2) Contextual mapping (Definition 2, condition 2) only requires separating context ids when the contexts genuinely differ ($\mathcal{V}^{(i)}
eq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}$ or $X_{:,k}^{(i)}
eq X_{:,l}^{(j)}$); without the $\mathcal{V}^{(i)}
eq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}$ hypothesis the step demands a strict gap even between identical tokens sitting in identical context sets, where no separation can or should hold. The correct condition adds both the absolute value and the hypothesis $\mathcal{V}^{(i)}
eq\mathcal{V}^{(j)}$.

